<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarmatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarmatism was the ideology and culture of the nobility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> from the sixteenth to the eighteenth centuries, based on the belief that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> descended from the ancient Sarmatians. It celebrated noble liberty, equality among nobles, Catholic piety and martial virtue, and shaped dress, portraiture and funeral customs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> critics blamed it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and xenophobia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarmatism.jpg" alt="Sarmatism" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanislaw_Antoni_Szczuka_(1652_1654-1710).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of a Sarmatian ancestry was set out by the chronicler Jan Długosz in the 15th century and continued by writers such as Marcin Kromer and Maciej Miechowita, whose Tractatus de Duabus Sarmatiis became a widely used reference work abroad. It arose from the humanists' admiration for Antiquity. According to Ptolemy's Geography, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a> covered the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and Tartary. The Sarmatians themselves were a confederacy of mostly Iranian tribes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> called descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>. Some writers held that the Sarmatians descended from Japheth, son of Noah.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were not alone in claiming an ancient people as ancestors: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took the Batavians, the French the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and the Hungarians the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Modern scholarship has found that the Alans, a late Sarmatian people, did invade Slavic lands before the sixth century and formed a ruling class that was gradually absorbed into the Slavic population, though a direct tie to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> remains uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sarmatism stressed equality among all nobles, horsemanship, village life and hospitality, and a culture of honour in which scars were prized. Nobles wore the żupan, the kontusz with its sash, and a curved sabre, dress derived from Ottoman models, and kept moustaches rather than beards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was widely spoken, if superficially. Funerals were elaborate, with a castrum doloris and portraits fixed to the coffin, an art form not found elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Politically it prized the Golden Liberty. Only nobles held the vote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and the sejmiki, the king was elected and his power limited, and from Siciński's veto each deputy could block a measure by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>. Adherents regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as the bulwark of Christendom. Religious freedom was recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-confederation">Warsaw Confederation</a> of 1573, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> brought intolerance: Arians were banished in 1658 and conversion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was outlawed ten years later. Sarmatism also drew Lithuanian and Ruthenian nobles into a common identity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the late 18th century the word carried negative associations. Publications such as Monitor mocked it as a name for unenlightened ideas, and Franciszek Zabłocki's play Sarmatyzm appeared in 1785. After the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> between 1772 and 1795, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> rehabilitated the old traditions, notably in Adam Mickiewicz's Pan Tadeusz of 1834 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henryk-sienkiewicz">Henryk Sienkiewicz</a>'s Trilogy of 1884 to 1888. In modern Polish, Sarmata is an ironic self-description.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarmatism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries the Polish nobility came to believe that it descended from the Sarmatians, an ideology known as <b>Sarmatism</b> that shaped its dress, portraiture and self-image.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
